WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.341
Client wants to send a payment request to a server at 10.10.2.20, on another network.

2
00:00:09.341 --> 00:00:15.044
Its routing table says the way there is through the router, at 10.10.1.2.

3
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:21.356
But a network card sends frames, and a frame is addressed to a card, by its MAC address.

4
00:00:21.356 --> 00:00:23.930
Client knows the router's IP address.

5
00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:25.600
It doesn't know its MAC.

6
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.993
The simplest fix is a file on every machine that lists each neighbour's IP and MAC.

7
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.839
It breaks the first time anything changes.

8
00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:41.813
The router's card was replaced last week, so the file names a card that's gone, and every card drops the frame.

9
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.361
So client asks.

10
00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:51.342
It broadcasts one question to the special address every card accepts: who has 10.10.1.2?

11
00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:52.793
Tell 10.10.1.10.

12
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:55.416
Every card on the link reads it.

13
00:00:55.416 --> 00:00:58.478
Peer isn't 10.10.1.2, so it stays quiet.

14
00:00:58.478 --> 00:01:03.760
The router is, so it answers straight back to client: here is my MAC.

15
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:10.116
Client keeps the answer in its ARP cache, so the next packets leave at once, with no question.

16
00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:14.281
Linux trusts the entry for 15 to 45 seconds, then checks again.

17
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.281
Now the frame is complete.

18
00:01:16.281 --> 00:01:19.499
Its MAC address names the router, the next hop.

19
00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:23.608
Its IP header still names the server, the final destination.

20
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.576
The router takes the frame and unwraps the packet.

21
00:01:27.576 --> 00:01:34.523
10.10.2.20 is on its other link, so it needs the server's MAC, and it finds it with ARP too.

22
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.952
It wraps the same packet in a new frame: from its own card, to the server's card.

23
00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:42.351
The IP addresses inside don't change.

24
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:48.939
MAC addresses change at every hop, and the IP destination stays the same the whole way.

25
00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:51.796
ARP is how each hop finds the next MAC.
